practiceProblems.description:
In this lesson, we learned about how to output **numbers** and **text** in C++ using cout statement.

To print numbers, we just add them after cout and << operator:
```cpp
cout << 21;

// Output:
// 21
```

Don't forget the semicolon (;) at the end of line.

Also, we can do mathematical operations inside cout statement itself, like addition, subtraction etc.
```c
cout << 2 + 2;
cout << 1 - 2;

// Output:
// 4
// -1
```

To print text, we use double quotes:
```c
cout << "I am learning C++";

// Output:
// I am learning C++
```

You get an error if you write any of the syntax incorrectly. Be careful when writing code, because machines need very exact instructions.

Also, any text that start with // is called a comment and C++ compiler ignores these lines when running your code.

In the next lesson, we will learn about how we can use print statement to print on multiple lines or multiple texts on same line, and do more complex mathematical operations.

